//! Prototype of `parallel!` (spec.md 5.4) lowered to explicit AVX2, for hand-written lane versions
//! of five item bodies. The scalar reference is `trame::__rt::parallel`, the function the macro
//! expands to. Nothing here is generated by trame; README.md says what a generator would have to do.
//!
//! Two lowerings, both exact with the scalar fold:
//!
//! * the window kernels take `W` consecutive items. If their keys are `k0, k0+1, ..` the slots are
//!   one contiguous load and store. If the keys are pairwise distinct the slots are a gather, the
//!   lane body, and `W` scalar stores. Otherwise the `W` items run through the scalar item in input
//!   order. Windows run in input order and nothing is reordered across them, so the result is the
//!   scalar fold's whatever the keys are, and so is the state at an abort.
//! * the register kernels hold every slot in vector registers, `n <= 8 * A` of them, and give each
//!   item to the lane whose id equals its key. They reorder nothing either: each lane sees its own
//!   key's items in input order.

#![allow(clippy::missing_safety_doc)]
#![allow(unused_unsafe)] // a safe intrinsic call in a target_feature fn needs no block; the macro-generated ones do

pub mod items;
pub mod plan;
#[cfg(target_arch = "x86_64")]
pub mod simd;

pub use items::*;
